\subsection{Motivation and gap}

Flexible job shop scheduling with transportation times conventionally reads travel times from a constant matrix indexed by location pairs~\cite{xin2025review}, as recent models still do~\cite{meng2025cp,qin2026knowledge,fu2025integrated}.
This is reasonable when vehicles rarely meet, but in real shops a few vehicles share sparse single-lane corridors, and at high fleet density their own traffic becomes the main cause of delay~\cite{kim2026congestion}.
Travel time then depends on each vehicle's path (conflict-free path planning, hereafter routing) and on the order of reservations on contended segments, so one job's transport can delay the operations of others.

Machine flexibility leaves room to optimize this delay: when the processing time depends on the chosen arm, the assignment selects both the destination and the duration.
On a constant matrix, choosing between a fast distant arm and a slower one near the load/unload point $v_0$ is arithmetic; under contention, the waiting on the corridor to the fast arm is invisible to the matrix, so the slower, better-connected arm may be better, depending on the congestion level.

Figure~\ref{fig:motivating} gives a recomputable instance: the focal job J1 may use a fast arm M1, reached only through an exclusive trunk that background jobs cross repeatedly, or a slow arm M2 on a separate branch.
Unimpeded, M1 is better by the smallest possible margin, and the constant matrix ranks it better ($C_{\max}=41$ against $54$); under conflict-free routing, J1's occupancy of the trunk makes the background jobs yield, and the ranking reverses ($95$ against $60$).
Only the evaluation has changed.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig/fig_motivating.pdf}
\Description{Left: shop network with an exclusive trunk to the fast arm. Right: makespans 41 versus 54 under the constant matrix and 95 versus 60 under conflict-free routing.}
\caption{Motivating instance ($4$ jobs, $2$ AGVs). (a)~Exclusive trunk ($\tau=6$) to the fast arm M1 ($t^P=3$), short corridor to the slow arm M2 ($t^P=14$), three M3-only jobs crossing the trunk; bottom: J1's unimpeded round trips ($19$ vs $20$). (b)~Instance makespan $C_{\max}$ under the constant matrix ($41$ vs $54$) and conflict-free routing ($95$ vs $60$). The reversal occurs in $205$ of $1200$ nearby parameter combinations.}
\label{fig:motivating}
%% Generated by fig/fig_motivating.py from clbs/output/motivating.json (same decoder as the paper).
\end{figure*}

Most existing work covers only part of this setting: scheduling with transportation uses a constant matrix, and conflict-free path planning takes tasks as given.
Van Os and Basten~\cite{vanos2026exact} formalize the combined problem and solve small benchmark instances to proven optimality.
We focus on settings not previously taken as a dedicated object of study: transport longer than processing ($\bar{T}_t/\bar{T}_p\approx4.0$, against at most \PubTtTpHi{} on common public instances), corridors exclusive over their whole length (van Os and Basten reserve nodes; Section~\ref{sec:fjsppt}), waiting only at berths, and congestion on a few shared corridors, at sizes that exact decomposition has difficulty solving.
There, heuristics schedule first and resolve conflicts afterwards~\cite{sun2023integrated}, feed back realized durations once without reassignment~\cite{li2026framework}, or embed routing in decoding without measuring its benefit and cost~\cite{lyu2019approach}.
Under equal compute it is thus unmeasured what counting yielding delay in the search gains when trading off ``fast but congested'' against ``slow but clear'' arms, and at what contention this gain is worth its cost.

\subsection{Approach and contributions}

The upper level of our closed-loop bilevel framework decides machine assignment and operation sequencing; the lower level plans conflict-free paths by time-window reservation.
On the evaluation path, routing-in-the-loop evaluation ranks each candidate by its makespan after conflict-free routing, so yielding wait enters the search objective.
Such a decode costs over an order of magnitude more than one on unimpeded times, and Li et al.\ list this fully iterative loop as future work without reporting its cost or gain.
On the decision path, reservation-table-aware dispatching trial-queries both legs (empty pickup, loaded delivery) of each candidate vehicle on the reservation table without writing the timetable, and selects the earliest feasible delivery instead of using idle-first dispatching~\cite{lu2022agv}; admissible lower-bound pruning and winner-path reuse skip only trial queries that cannot affect the result.

A four-level baseline ladder forms a $2\times2$ design: whether the search counts conflict-induced delay, and whether vehicles are chosen by unimpeded time or by trial query.
B0 is the open-loop structure (schedule on unimpeded times, then execute by conflict-free routing) of constant-matrix methods and Li et al.; B0+ dispatches by the reservation table on a frozen schedule, as in Sun et al.; B1 closes the loop with rule-based dispatching; B2 is our method.
All share the search, the router and the wall-clock budget, and are compared by realized makespan under the same router (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}).

With dispatching fixed, the closed loop reduces makespan by \GainLoopRule{} ($p\PLoopRule$); within it, reservation-table-aware dispatching reduces it by a further \GainProbeLoop{} ($p\PProbeLoop$); end to end, the reduction is \GainEndToEnd{} ($p\PEndToEnd$). The baselines differ, so these do not add.
Open-loop dispatching yields only \GainProbeOpen{}, and the end-to-end gain is slightly below the \GainIndepProduct{} predicted by independent composition.
After Holm correction, the closed loop remains significant on \CellsSigLoopAdj{} of the \NCases{} instances, rising roughly with contention, whereas dispatching is significant on none, and \NSeeds{} seeds per instance are not sufficient to identify where it is stably positive (Section~\ref{sec:whichcongestion}).
Our contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Controlled instances.} Instance families with controllable contention intensity let us examine how high contention must be before counting yielding wait brings a gain (Section~\ref{sec:instances}).
\item \textbf{Routing-in-the-loop evaluation.} Its gain and cost over the open loop are measured at an equal wall-clock budget, and every chromosome decodes into a conflict-free, deadlock-free timetable without repair (Proposition~\ref{prop:decodable}).
\item \textbf{Low-overhead dispatching.} Two speed-ups change neither the selected vehicle nor the final timetable (Proposition~\ref{prop:speedup_equiv}), which makes a net gain possible at an equal budget.
\item \textbf{Decomposed evidence.} The closed loop is the larger effect and dispatching a much smaller one, and the two do not amplify each other (Section~\ref{sec:main}). An extra cost on occupied corridor--time slots (hereafter, surcharge) and yield-record-guided reassignment or staggering only add overhead; the failure of the surcharge suggests first checking whether the receiving level's objective already contains what an interface would pass.
\end{enumerate}

Sections~\ref{sec:Chapter_II}--\ref{sec:Chapter_V} cover related work, the problem (notation in Table~\ref{tab:notation}), the framework and the experiments; the final section concludes.


\section{Related work}
\label{sec:Chapter_II}

Table~\ref{tab:related} compares the related work with this paper.

\subsection{Two strands}

Flexible job shop scheduling with transportation (FJSP\_PT) brings material movement into the schedule (FJSP review~\cite{dauzereperes2024fjsp}; benchmarks~\cite{homayouni2021production}), and its dominant convention is a constant travel-time matrix~\cite{xin2025review}.
Recent work keeps the matrix and improves solution methods: Meng et al.~\cite{meng2025cp} close 29 open benchmark instances and improve 27 best-known solutions with constraint programming and a genetic algorithm; Qin et al.~\cite{qin2026knowledge} use Q-learning and a local search guided by AGVs and the critical path; Fu et al.~\cite{fu2025integrated} decide production and logistics jointly.
Xin et al.\ note that such assumptions ``reduce the complexity of the problem and even change its nature'', that instances ``differ mainly in size'', and that $\bar{T}_t/\bar{T}_p$ is highly likely to affect algorithm performance.
In this strand vehicles never delay one another, and although critical-chain local search is mature~\cite{otala2021graph,tamssaouet2023neighborhood}, a critical path on the matrix leads back to an operation or a machine, never to a corridor.
% [ref_check: key=meng2025cp; claim="closes 29 open instances, improves 27 best-known solutions"; need=quote]

Optimal multi-agent path finding (MAPF) is NP-hard~\cite{gao2023mapf}, and Kasaura et al.~\cite{kasaura2022sipp} extend Safe Interval Path Planning to continuous-time road networks.
For continuously arriving tasks, Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2024guidance} optimize edge weights offline to steer agents away from bottlenecks, and Kim and Kim~\cite{kim2026congestion} propose congestion-aware dispatching rules for large AGV fleets; surveys~\cite{lu2022agv,fragapane2021planning} treat dispatching and path planning as separate layers.
Here tasks are inputs, so the router cannot revise the assignment, and indirect congestion measures (local density, offline edge weights) cannot identify which transport caused yielding, when, and where.

\subsection{Integration and our position}

The logic-based Benders decomposition of Van Os and Basten~\cite{vanos2026exact} returns conflicts from an integer-programming routing subproblem to a constraint-programming master as cuts, solving most benchmarks to proven optimality and improving about half by at least 10\% over prior heuristics, at small scale; they list fuller use of ``which conflicts caused the infeasibility'' as future work.
Sun et al.~\cite{sun2023integrated} resolve conflicts by time-window Dijkstra after a hybrid genetic algorithm has fixed the schedule; Li et al.~\cite{li2026framework} feed realized durations back once, shifting start times of the current solution without reassignment; Lyu et al.~\cite{lyu2019approach} route inside genetic-algorithm decoding to size the fleet on small instances, without an open-loop comparison or a cost report.
The gap thus lies not in whether conflicts are handled but in how routing results enter the schedule and what that is worth: beyond the reach of exact decomposition, we route every candidate, dispatch by trial query, measure both contributions at an equal wall-clock budget, and vary contention intensity, since instances with the same $\bar{T}_t/\bar{T}_p$ may differ greatly in how much congestion reassignment can bypass.

